# Issue #8: RFC: Decision authority, mission modes, evidence maturity, and stagnation escalation
# 
# ## Summary
# 
# Explore a protocol-level extension for four orthogonal semantics that are currently implicit in Agent Relay:
# 
# 1. **decision authority** — who is allowed to decide *what*;
# 2. **mission mode** — what kind of work a pass is actually performing;
# 3. **claim/evidence maturity** — how strongly a relied-upon claim is established;
# 4. **stagnation escalation** — when repeated failure should trigger a role/lens change instead of another same-role retry.
# 
# A fifth concept, **planning-context completeness**, is included as an exploratory/non-normative mechanism for deciding how much execution autonomy is safe without rating or profiling the human user.
# 
# This RFC is informed by Anthropic's June 16, 2026 research report, ["Agentic coding and persistent returns to expertise"](https://www.anthropic.com/research/claude-code-expertise), based on a privacy-preserving analysis of ~400,000 Claude Code sessions between October 2025 and April 2026.
# 
# The useful lesson is not that Agent Relay should score user expertise or optimize for longer autonomous action chains. The useful lesson is that effective agentic work separates **planning authority**, **execution autonomy**, and **verifiable success**.
# 
# Agent Relay already has the primitives needed to make those distinctions explicit: five stable roles, Reviewer lenses, mutation boundaries, immutable snapshots, evidence hierarchy, Verifier checkpoints, finding lifecycle, and Integrator readiness authority.
# 
# ## Relationship to RFC #6
# 
# This RFC is deliberately separate from #6, **Runtime adapters and bounded iterative review loops**.
# 
# The intended boundary is:
# 
# > **RFC #6 governs control flow. This RFC governs decision semantics.**
# 
# RFC #6 asks:
# 
# - how repeated passes execute;
# - how review cycles are bounded;
# - how runtime state, cancellation, budgets, and termination work;
# - how external runtimes/adapters may execute Agent Relay passes.
# 
# This RFC asks:
# 
# - who owns mission, architecture, implementation, verification interpretation, and readiness decisions;
# - what category of work is occurring;
# - how strong the evidence for a claim actually is;
# - when repeated failure requires escalation or replanning.
# 
# A runtime or bounded review cycle defined by #6 MAY consume these semantics, but should not define them.
# 
# ## Prior art / research signal
# 
# Anthropic's report describes several observations that map cleanly onto Agent Relay protocol concerns:
# 
# ### Planning versus execution decisions
# 
# The report separates meaningful session decisions into:
# 
# - **planning** — what to do, which approach to take, what counts as done;
# - **execution** — which files to change, what code to write, which commands to run.
# 
# In the observed sessions, users made about 70% of planning decisions while Claude made about 80% of execution decisions.
# 
# For Agent Relay, the important implication is:
# 
# > **Execution autonomy does not imply planning authority.**
# 
# An agent may be allowed to autonomously edit many files, run commands, or operate a deployment while remaining prohibited from changing the mission, architecture, acceptance criteria, or definition of done.
# 
# ### Nine work modes
# 
# Anthropic classifies sessions into nine work modes:
# 
# - building;
# - fixing;
# - testing;
# - orchestrating;
# - operating software;
# - understanding;
# - planning;
# - analyzing data;
# - communicating / document work.
# 
# These should not become Agent Relay roles. They describe the **kind of work**, not the responsibility/authority model.
# 
# ### Verified success
# 
# Anthropic distinguishes transcript-judged success from **verified success**. Their strict measure requires judged success plus at least one hard verifiable signal, such as matching Git activity, a passing test suite, or other strong evidence.
# 
# Agent Relay already says evidence beats assertions and has an evidence hierarchy. The opportunity is to make the **maturity of a specific relied-upon claim** explicit and portable across passes.
# 
# ### Recovery from trouble
# 
# The report treats errors, failed tests, repeated attempts, and user pushback as failure/trouble signals. Sessions with greater task-specific expertise recover more often from trouble.
# 
# Agent Relay should not score or simulate human expertise. It can, however, encode the useful recovery behavior: repeated failed attempts or contradictory evidence should trigger a **protocol escalation**, not indefinite same-role retries.
# 
# ### Task-specific expertise
# 
# Anthropic explicitly treats expertise as task-specific rather than occupational or general ability. Their classifier looks at framing precision, requested verification, and whether the user corrects the agent or vice versa.
# 
# Agent Relay should not adopt a `novice/intermediate/expert` user rating. The actionable substitute is to measure whether the **planning context required for safe autonomous execution is complete**.
# 
# ## Design principle
# 
# **Separate responsibility, scrutiny, work type, decision authority, and evidence strength.**
# 
# The existing axes remain:
# 
# ```text
# Role
#   = who is responsible for the pass
# 
# Reviewer lens
#   = what a Reviewer scrutinizes
# ```
# 
# This RFC proposes additional orthogonal axes:
# 
# ```text
# Mission mode
#   = what kind of work is being performed
# 
# Decision authority
#   = which decisions this actor/pass may make
# 
# Evidence maturity
#   = how strongly a relied-upon claim is established
# 
# Escalation state
#   = whether continuing the same route is still justified
# ```
# 
# None of these should create a sixth role.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## Proposed capability 1: decision authority envelope
# 
# Add a protocol concept describing **which classes of decisions are delegated to which actor/role for the current mission**.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# Decision authority:
# - Mission / desired outcome: User
# - Architecture / acceptance criteria: User + Integrator
# - Implementation mechanics: Builder
# - Environment procedure: Executor
# - Evidence interpretation: Verifier
# - Readiness / reconciliation: Integrator
# ```
# 
# This is not an ACL system and does not grant tool permissions. It records the semantic delegation al
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Agent Relay RFC #8 demo: decision authority, mission modes, evidence maturity, and stagnation escalation.

Models four orthogonal semantics as node archetypes with typed edges, and uses walkers to
exercise the protocol-level concerns the RFC raises: traversal of delegated decision authority,
escalation on repeated same-role failure, and evidence-maturity gating.
"""

# --- Mission modes (the "kind of work" axis) ---

node Mission {
    has title: str = "";
    has desired_outcome: str = "";
}

node MissionMode {
    has name: str = "";
    has category: str = "";
}

# --- Decision authority envelope ---

node DecisionClass {
    has label: str = "";
    # Shared field so a union trigger can read a description from any
    # participant in the envelope/mission subgraph.
    has scope: str = "";
}

edge Delegates {
    has actor_role: str = "";
}

edge BelongsTo {
    has mission_title: str = "";
}

# --- Evidence maturity ---

node Evidence {
    has claim: str = "";
    has maturity: str = "unverified";
    has verifiable_signal: bool = False;
}

edge ReliesOn {
    has relying_pass: str = "";
}

# --- Stagnation / escalation ---

node FailureRecord {
    has pass_id: str = "";
    has role: str = "";
    has attempt: int = 0;
}

edge FiledAgainst {
    has target: str = "";
}

edge EscalatesTo {
    has reason: str = "";
}

glob ESCALATION_THRESHOLD: int = 3;

# =====================================================================
# Walkers
# =====================================================================

# AuthorityInspector: traverses the envelope and reports which actor owns
# each DecisionClass. Demonstrates "execution autonomy does not imply
# planning authority" by separating the delegation classes.
walker AuthorityInspector {
    has audit_log: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:BelongsTo:->];
    }

    can cross_class with DecisionClass entry {
        for n in [->:Delegates:->] {
            self.audit_log.append(f"{here.label} -> {n.actor_role}");
        }
        visit [->:Delegates:->];
    }

    can record_actor with DecisionClass entry {
        print(f"[AUTH] {here.label} is delegated via scope '{here.scope}'");
        visit [->:BelongsTo:->] else { disengage; };
    }
}

# EscalationWalker: walks the failure trail for a given role and decides
# whether the same-role retry loop should escalate. Models "repeated
# failure should trigger a role/lens change".
walker EscalationWalker {
    has role: str = "";
    has failed: list[str] = [];
    has escalated: bool = False;
    has new_role: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:FiledAgainst:->];
    }

    can collect with FailureRecord entry {
        if here.role == self.role {
            self.failed.append(here.pass_id);
        }
        visit [->:FiledAgainst:->];
    }

    can decide with FailureRecord entry {
        count = len(self.failed);
        verdict = "OK" if count < ESCALATION_THRESHOLD else "ESCALATE";
        self.escalated = count >= ESCALATION_THRESHOLD;
        if self.escalated {
            self.new_role = "Reviewer" if self.role == "Builder" else "Integrator";
        }
        reason = f"{count} same-role failures for role '{self.role}'";
        suffix = f", escalate to {self.new_role}" if self.escalated else "";
        print(f"[ESCALATION] {reason} -> verdict {verdict}{suffix}");
        visit [->:EscalatesTo:->] else { disengage; };
    }
}

# EvidenceAuditor: walks every Evidence item reachable from root and
# reports the weakest relied-upon evidence. Models "evidence maturity":
# planning authority should not be granted on unverified evidence.
walker EvidenceAuditor {
    has weakest: str = "verified";
    has weakest_claim: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [->:ReliesOn:->];
    }

    can audit with Evidence entry {
        rank = 0;
        if here.maturity == "verified" {
            rank = 2;
        } elif here.maturity == "partial" {
            rank = 1;
        } else {
            rank = 0;
        }
        current_rank = 0;
        if self.weakest == "verified" {
            current_rank = 2;
        } elif self.weakest == "partial" {
            current_rank = 1;
        } else {
            current_rank = 0;
        }
        if rank < current_rank {
            self.weakest = here.maturity;
            self.weakest_claim = here.claim;
        }
        visit [->:ReliesOn:->];
    }

    can finalize with Evidence entry {
        safe = self.weakest == "verified";
        verdict = "GRANTED" if safe else "DENIED";
        line = "[EVIDENCE] weakest maturity: " + self.weakest
               + " on claim '" + self.weakest_claim + "'"
               + " -> planning authority " + verdict;
        print(line);
        visit [->:ReliesOn:->] else { disengage; };
    }
}

# =====================================================================
# Demonstration graph
# =====================================================================

with entry {
    # --- Mission + decision classes ---
    mission = Mission(title="Ship agent relay v2",
                       desired_outcome="Protocol extension RFC #8 demo");

    dc_mission = DecisionClass(label="mission / desired outcome", scope="rfc8-demo");
    dc_arch    = DecisionClass(label="architecture / acceptance criteria", scope="rfc8-demo");
    dc_impl    = DecisionClass(label="implementation mechanics", scope="rfc8-demo");
    dc_ready   = DecisionClass(label="readiness / reconciliation", scope="rfc8-demo");

    # Typed-edge wiring with role payloads. Mirrors the RFC: User owns
    # mission, User+Integrator share architecture, Builder owns mechanics,
    # Integrator owns readiness — separating planning from execution.
    mission +>:BelongsTo(mission_title="Ship agent relay v2"):+> dc_mission;
    mission +>:BelongsTo(mission_title="Ship agent relay v2"):+> dc_arch;
    mission +>:BelongsTo(mission_title="Ship agent relay v2"):+> dc_impl;
    mission +>:BelongsTo(mission_title="Ship agent relay v2"):+> dc_ready;

    mission +>:Delegates(actor_role="User"):+>          dc_mission;
    mission +>:Delegates(actor_role="User+Integrator"):+> dc_arch;
    mission +>:Delegates(actor_role="Builder"):+>        dc_impl;
    mission +>:Delegates(actor_role="Integrator"):+>     dc_ready;

    # Mission-mode annotation (the "kind of work" axis).
    mode = MissionMode(name="pass-7", category="building");
    root ++> mission ++> mode;

    # --- Evidence trail: a relied-upon claim with one weak link ---
    ev_strong = Evidence(claim="git commit present", maturity="verified", verifiable_signal=True);
    ev_weak   = Evidence(claim="user said it works",  maturity="unverified", verifiable_signal=False);
    root +>:ReliesOn(relying_pass="pass-7"):+> ev_strong;
    root +>:ReliesOn(relying_pass="pass-7"):+> ev_weak;

    # --- Stagnation trail: a Builder failing repeatedly ---
    root ++> FailureRecord(pass_id="p1", role="Builder", attempt=1)
        +>:FiledAgainst(target="pass-7"):+> FailureRecord(pass_id="p2", role="Builder", attempt=2)
        +>:FiledAgainst(target="pass-7"):+> FailureRecord(pass_id="p3", role="Builder", attempt=3);

    # --- Spawn the three walkers ---
    AuthorityInspector() spawn root;
    EvidenceAuditor() spawn root;
    EscalationWalker(role="Builder") spawn root;
}

