# A maintainer on the project reussir-lang/reussir (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:
# 
# Issue #542: [build][ci] git-restore-mtime + cached cargo target/ can silently skip build scripts, embedding stale generated content
# 
# ## Summary
# 
# Every CI workflow in this repo runs `chetan/git-restore-mtime-action` alongside a restored cargo `target/` directory (`actions-rust-lang/setup-rust-toolchain`, which bundles `Swatinem/rust-cache`). That combination can make cargo **silently skip a build script whose declared inputs have changed**, so the build embeds stale generated content and succeeds.
# 
# Cargo's build-script freshness check compares **mtimes, not content**. `git-restore-mtime` rewrites source mtimes to their last *commit* time; a restored `target/` carries fingerprints written when the cache was *saved*, which is later. Cargo sees the output as newer than its inputs and calls the script fresh.
# 
# ## Reproduction
# 
# Minimal crate: `build.rs` declares `cargo::rerun-if-changed=inputs`, reads `inputs/data.txt`, writes it to `OUT_DIR/baked.txt`; `main.rs` does `include_str!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/baked.txt"))`.
# 
# ```
# 1. content = VERSION_ONE, build                     -> binary prints VERSION_ONE
# 2. content = VERSION_TWO, mtime backdated to 2020   -> binary prints VERSION_ONE   ← BUG: contents changed, stale bytes embedded
# 3. content unchanged,     mtime = now               -> binary prints VERSION_TWO   ← control: same content as (2), only mtime differs
# ```
# 
# Step 3 is the control that makes step 2 meaningful: the content in steps 2 and 3 is identical, and only the mtime differs — so mtime alone decides whether the script re-runs.
# 
# ### Second layer: identical output still forces recompiles
# 
# ```
# rm -rf target/debug/build && cargo build   ->  Compiling <crate>
# rm -rf target/debug/build && cargo build   ->  Compiling <crate>   (content unchanged between runs)
# ```
# 
# The consuming crate rebuilds even when the regenerated `OUT_DIR` bytes are byte-identical, because the dependency check on the generated file is mtime-based too. **"Identical output" protects nothing anywhere in this chain.**
# 
# ## Why this is live here, not theoretical
# 
# Four build scripts:
# 
# ```
# crates/rene/build.rs
# crates/reussir-backend-sys/build.rs
# crates/reussir-backend/build.rs
# crates/reussir-core/build.rs
# ```
# 
# `crates/rene/build.rs` is the sharpest case: it bakes a zstd-compressed tar of the runtime source into the binary (`RT_BUNDLE`), which `rene build` later materializes and compiles. A skipped run means `rene` ships a **source tree older than the repo's** and reports itself up to date, correctly by its own logic.
# 
# Note that `rene`'s own design is already content-based and deliberately so — its archive is built with sorted entries and fixed metadata precisely so the bytes depend only on the sources, and the unpacked directory is keyed on `bundle_hash()`. The defect is not in that design. It is that the layer beneath it decides whether to regenerate the archive from timestamps.
# 
# `crates/reussir-core/build.rs` declares no `rerun-if-changed` at all, so cargo falls back to the package's files — still mtime-based.
# 
# ## What this does *not* explain
# 
# This is **not** the cause of the aarch64 `std_hash_test.rr` link failure, and should not be cited as such. That hypothesis was investigated and ruled out: `crates/rene/CMakeLists.txt` sets `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/target`, so `rene` is built into `build/target`, which the rust-cache does not cover (its `Cache Paths` list the workspace `target` only). `rene` is built fresh on every CI run.
# 
# The defect above is real on its own evidence and worth fixing independently.
# 
# ## Fix options
# 
# **A — put a content hash of the build script's declared inputs into the rust-cache key.**
# Measured miss rate for `rene`'s five declared inputs (`crates/reussir-rt/{src,Cargo.toml}`, workspace `Cargo.toml`, workspace `Cargo.lock`, `library/core`), commits on `main` since 2026-07-07:
# 
# ```
# union of the five        : 31
# total sampled (API cap)  : 100
# ```
# 
# ≈31%, dominated by `Cargo.lock` (17). All five genuinely affect the baked bytes — the lockfile is written into the tarball verbatim and the workspace manifest is folded into the generated standalone manifest, so none can be excluded. **Cost on a miss: a full cold rebuild.**
# 
# **B — remove `target/<profile>/build/` after restoring the cache.**
# Verified to force re-runs correctly. Note that deleting only `.fingerprint/run-build-script*` does **not** work — cargo still reuses the previous output. **Cost: every build-script consumer recompiles on every run**, and that set includes `reussir-core` and `reussir-backend`.
# 
# So the comparison is `0.31 × full cold rebuild` against `1.00 × build-script-consumer rebuild`. Wall-clock numbers, not commit ratios, should decide it.
# 
# Removing `git-restore-mtime` is a third option but a larger change: it exists to prevent spurious rebuilds, and dropping it would plausibly defeat the `target/` cache entirely.
# 
# ## Principle
# 
# Freshness decisions in this build should key on **content, not time**. The `reussir-backend-sys` archive fingerprint (#540, merged via #539) is one instance of this repair; this is the same principle one layer up.
# 
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Build-script freshness model.

Models the cargo build-script freshness scenario from issue #542:
build scripts compare mtimes (not content) of their declared inputs
against the cached output, so a restored cache + backdated source
mtimes can make cargo silently skip a script whose contents actually
changed, embedding stale generated content into the final binary.

Entities and relationships are lifted into native Jac archetypes:

    node BuildScript    — a cargo build.rs (rene, reussir-core, ...)
    node SourceFile     — a declared rerun-if-changed input
    node GeneratedOut   — an OUT_DIR artifact (e.g. baked.txt, RT_BUNDLE)
    node Binary         — the final linked binary (e.g. rene)

Edges carry the meaning of the relationship:
    Declares  (BuildScript -> SourceFile)   rerun-if-changed=...
    Produces  (BuildScript -> GeneratedOut) script writes this
    Embeds    (Binary -> GeneratedOut)       include_str!/include_bytes!
    Freshness (SourceFile -> GeneratedOut)  the mtime comparison cargo runs

A walker walks the freshness edges in dependency order, applying the
three diagnostics the issue calls out: stale-mtime-skip, identical-
output-still-recompiles, and content-vs-time mismatch. The walker
carries an explicit visited set because cycle policy is observable
domain logic, not scaffolding.
"""

node BuildScript {
    has name: str = "";
    has declares_rerun: bool = False;
}

node SourceFile {
    has path: str = "";
    has content_hash: str = "";
    has mtime: int = 0;
}

node GeneratedOut {
    has path: str = "";
    has baked_hash: str = "";
    has out_mtime: int = 0;
    has src_mtime: int = 0;
}

node Binary {
    has name: str = "";
    has embedded_hash: str = "";
}

edge Declares {}
edge Produces {}
edge Embeds {}
edge Freshness {}

walker FreshnessAudit {
    has visited: list = [];
    has findings: list = [];

    def seen(n: str) -> bool {
        return n in self.visited;
    }

    can start with Root entry {
        self.visited = [];
        visit [-->];
    }

    can audit with BuildScript entry {
        print(f"[audit] build script: {here.name}  rerun-if-changed={here.declares_rerun}");
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        visit [->:Declares:->];
    }

    can check_input with SourceFile entry {
        if self.seen(here.path) {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.path);
        print(f"  input  {here.path}  content={here.content_hash}  mtime={here.mtime}");
        visit [->:Freshness:->];
    }

    can compare with GeneratedOut entry {
        if self.seen(here.path) {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.path);
        stale_skip = here.out_mtime > here.src_mtime;
        verdict = "STALE-SKIP" if stale_skip else "FRESH";
        print(f"  out    {here.path}  baked={here.baked_hash}  out_mtime={here.out_mtime}  src_mtime={here.src_mtime}  -> {verdict}");
        self.findings.append(f"{here.path}:{verdict}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker StaleEmbed {
    has visited: list = [];

    def seen(n: str) -> bool {
        return n in self.visited;
    }

    can start with Root entry {
        self.visited = [];
        visit [-->];
    }

    can trace with Binary entry {
        if self.seen(here.name) {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.name);
        print(f"[embed] binary {here.name} carries {here.embedded_hash}");
        visit [->:Embeds:->];
    }

    can check with GeneratedOut entry {
        if self.seen(here.path) {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        self.visited.append(here.path);
        print(f"  embedded bytes = {here.baked_hash}  (binary reports itself up-to-date by its own logic)");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # The four build scripts the issue names.
    rene = BuildScript(name="rene/build.rs", declares_rerun=True);
    backend_sys = BuildScript(name="reussir-backend-sys/build.rs", declares_rerun=True);
    backend = BuildScript(name="reussir-backend/build.rs", declares_rerun=True);
    core = BuildScript(name="reussir-core/build.rs", declares_rerun=False);

    # Inputs rene declares (the five the issue enumerates). mtime backdated.
    rt_src = SourceFile(path="crates/reussir-rt/src", content_hash="VERSION_TWO", mtime=2020);
    rt_manifest = SourceFile(path="crates/reussir-rt/Cargo.toml", content_hash="V2", mtime=2020);
    ws_manifest = SourceFile(path="Cargo.toml", content_hash="V2", mtime=2020);
    ws_lock = SourceFile(path="Cargo.lock", content_hash="V2", mtime=2020);
    lib_core = SourceFile(path="library/core", content_hash="V2", mtime=2020);

    # Generated outputs the build scripts bake. out_mtime is later (cache-restored).
    rt_bundle = GeneratedOut(path="OUT_DIR/RT_BUNDLE", baked_hash="VERSION_ONE", out_mtime=2024, src_mtime=2020);
    core_gen = GeneratedOut(path="OUT_DIR/core.ir", baked_hash="V2", out_mtime=2024, src_mtime=2020);

    # Final binaries.
    rene_bin = Binary(name="rene", embedded_hash="VERSION_ONE");
    backend_bin = Binary(name="reussir-backend", embedded_hash="V2");

    root ++> rene ++> backend_sys ++> backend ++> core;

    # Declares: each script lists its inputs.
    rene +>:Declares:+> rt_src;
    rene +>:Declares:+> rt_manifest;
    rene +>:Declares:+> ws_manifest;
    rene +>:Declares:+> ws_lock;
    rene +>:Declares:+> lib_core;
    core +>:Declares:+> ws_manifest;

    # Produces: each script writes its OUT_DIR artifact.
    rene +>:Produces:+> rt_bundle;
    core +>:Produces:+> core_gen;

    # Embeds: the binary include_str!'s / include_bytes!'s the generated file.
    rene_bin +>:Embeds:+> rt_bundle;
    backend_bin +>:Embeds:+> core_gen;

    # Freshness: the mtime comparison cargo runs per script per input.
    rene +>:Freshness:+> rt_bundle;
    core +>:Freshness:+> core_gen;

    print("=== FreshnessAudit (the bug the issue names) ===");
    audit = FreshnessAudit();
    root spawn audit;
    stale = 0;
    for f in audit.findings {
        if f.endswith(":STALE-SKIP") {
            stale = stale + 1;
        }
    }
    print(f"summary: {stale} stale-skip findings out of {len(audit.findings)}");
    print("note: reussir-core declares NO rerun-if-changed, so cargo falls back to package files (still mtime-based)");

    print("");
    print("=== StaleEmbed (the second layer: binary embeds stale bytes) ===");
    root spawn StaleEmbed();
}

