# So a composite action whose steps reference the same manifests re-parses them once per path, and the work is `fanout^depth` rather than proportional to the number of files. Measured at depth 9, one `action.yml` per level:
# 
# | fanout | time |
# | --- | --- |
# | 2 | 11 ms |
# | 3 | 181 ms |
# | 4 | 1.59 s |
# | 5 | 8.72 s |
# 
# Roughly 8x per increment, from about ten files of a few hundred bytes each. `ctx` is threaded through the recursion but never checked, so a caller's timeout or cancellation does not stop the work once it starts.
# 
# This runs wherever Deputy inventories a repository it did not write, which includes `deputy scan` on an arbitrary target and PR CI on a fork's changes.
# 
# ## Proposal
# 
# - Extend the visited set to cover action manifests, not just workflows. A manifest parsed once should not be parsed again within the same extraction, regardless of how many steps reference it. This alone converts the growth from exponential to linear in file count.
# - Add a global node/work budget for the extraction, independent of depth, and stop with a partial result plus a warning rather than running to completion.
# - Check `ctx.Err()` at each recursion step so cancellation and deadlines take effect.
# 
# Worth confirming whether the same shape exists in other recursive extractors while fixing this one.
# 
# ## Preventing recurrence
# 
# - A benchmark or bounded-time test built from a synthetic fan-out fixture, asserting the extraction completes within a fixed budget. A depth-9, fanout-5 fixture is a few hundred bytes and currently takes ~9 seconds, so the assertion is unambiguous.
# - The rule worth stating for extractors generally: any traversal over attacker-supplied file structure needs (a) memoization keyed on the resource, (b) a global work budget, and (c) context checks. Depth limits alone do not bound work when the branching factor is attacker-controlled.
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Composite action manifest extractor with cycle safety and a work budget.

Models the GitHub actions recursion pain from issue #97: a manifest may
reference other manifests in a tree of any shape, so naive recursion is
``fanout ** depth`` work. The walker (1) memoizes manifests it has already
parsed, (2) checks a global work budget and disengages with a partial
report, and (3) honors a per-step cancel signal — all of which are domain
logic that the original Go code was missing.
"""

# --- Entities ---------------------------------------------------------------

node Manifest {
    has name: str = "";
    has fanout: int = 0;        # how many children this manifest references
    has depth: int = 0;         # distance from the root invocation
    has parsed: bool = False;   # flipped to True after a successful parse
}

# --- Typed edges ------------------------------------------------------------

# A ``Steps`` edge is the typed "this action step references that manifest"
# relationship that the original parser followed by hand.
edge Steps {
    has slot: int = 0;
}

# --- Walker -----------------------------------------------------------------

walker Extractor {
    has max_depth: int = 8;
    has budget: int = 64;            # global node-visit cap
    has cancel: bool = False;       # caller-side cancellation flag

    can start with Root entry {
        # First manifest is the entrypoint: parse it, then walk its Steps.
        here ++> Manifest(name="action.yml", fanout=3, depth=0);
        visit [-->];
    }

    can parse with Manifest entry {
        # (a) Context check: a cancelled caller stops the walk immediately.
        if self.cancel {
            print(f"[cancel] aborting before parsing {here.name} at depth {here.depth}");
            disengage;
        }

        # (b) Memoization: a manifest parsed once is never re-parsed.
        if here.parsed {
            print(f"[memo] skip already-parsed {here.name} at depth {here.depth}");
            visit [-->] else { disengage; }
            return;
        }

        # (c) Work budget: stop with a partial result instead of running away.
        if self.budget <= 0 {
            print(f"[budget] exhausted, partial result at {here.name} (depth {here.depth})");
            disengage;
        }
        self.budget -= 1;

        # (d) Depth cap: the only bound the original code had.
        if here.depth >= self.max_depth {
            print(f"[depth] cap {self.max_depth} hit at {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }

        here.parsed = True;
        print(f"[parse] {here.name} (depth {here.depth}, fanout {here.fanout}, "
              f"budget left {self.budget})");

        # Recurse into each child manifest over the typed ``Steps`` edge.
        for i in range(here.fanout) {
            child = Manifest(
                name=f"{here.name}/step{i}",
                fanout=3,
                depth=here.depth + 1,
            );
            here +>:Steps(slot=i):+> child;
        }
        visit [->:Steps:->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

# --- Demo graph -------------------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    print("--- run 1: healthy traversal (small fanout, deep budget) ---");
    root spawn Extractor(max_depth=8, budget=64);

    print("\n--- run 2: same graph, tight work budget (10) ---");
    # Re-create the entrypoint because the previous walker consumed it.
    root ++> Manifest(name="action.yml", fanout=3, depth=0);
    root spawn Extractor(max_depth=8, budget=10);

    print("\n--- run 3: pre-cancelled before any work happens ---");
    root ++> Manifest(name="action.yml", fanout=3, depth=0);
    root spawn Extractor(max_depth=8, budget=64, cancel=True);
}

