# 
# Issue #584: Composite fan-out expands unbounded in practice: single composites yielding six-figure child row counts
# 
# ## Summary
# 
# `CompositeInboxFanout` expands a received composite into child inbox rows. Composites carrying
# **tens of thousands** of inner events are reaching consumers and being expanded, one of them into
# **over 200,000 child rows** from a single parent. A cap exists — `MaxInnerEventsAllowed`, with a
# dedicated `MessageFailureReason.CompositeInnerEventLimitExceeded` — but it is evidently not binding
# on this path.
# 
# Observed on two independent consumers of the same bulk workload:
# 
# | consumer | composites observed | largest single expansion |
# |---|---|---|
# | A | ~170 | ~53,000 child rows |
# | B | ~355 | **~200,000 child rows** |
# 
# Consumer B's inbox grew to roughly a million unprocessed rows, of which effectively 100% descended
# from a small number of these composites. Its inbox climbed for hours **with no producer running and
# an empty broker** — the growth was entirely local expansion of work it already held.
# 
# ## Why this is worse than a large batch
# 
# The expansion is a write amplification that happens INSIDE the consumer, after admission control has
# already accepted the message. From the outside the consumer looks like it is receiving traffic that
# does not exist:
# 
# - broker subscriptions empty, no upstream producing, yet inbox depth rising by tens of thousands per
#   minute
# - the composite row itself remains unprocessed while its children are written, so a depth-based
#   health check sees a growing backlog and no obvious source
# - a service can be pushed from a manageable queue into a multi-hundred-thousand-row backlog by
#   admitting a handful of messages
# 
# It also defeats every downstream bound. Claim windows, outstanding-row budgets and lease sizing are
# all calibrated against inbox rows, and one admitted message can add six figures of them.
# 
# ## Where the cap appears not to apply
# 
# `CompositeInboxFanout.TryExpand` checks `composite.MaxInnerEventsAllowed` on both the materialized
# and raw-inner paths, and the limit is per-composite rather than global. Two things worth checking:
# 
# 1. whether the default value of `MaxInnerEventsAllowed` on the composite types involved is high
#    enough to be effectively unbounded
# 2. whether the raw-carry path (children built directly from stored JSON) reaches the same check with
#    the same value
# 
# ## Suggested direction
# 
# - **A bound that reflects consumer capacity, not just message validity.** A composite whose
#   expansion would exceed what the consumer can lease and drain should be rejected or chunked, not
#   expanded in one shot.
# - **Chunked expansion.** Expanding a large composite incrementally — with the parent retained until
#   its children are drained — would keep depth bounded and let backpressure work.
# - **A log line at expansion.** Nothing currently reports "expanded composite X into N children",
#   which is why a consumer growing by tens of thousands of rows per minute has no attributable cause
#   in its own logs. This alone would have made the behavior obvious immediately.
# 
# ## Note
# 
# This compounds with the auto-repair storm in #582: redelivery requests bundle their windows into
# composites, so a repair loop produces exactly the oversized composites this issue describes. The two
# failures amplify each other — but the composite bounding problem is independent and reproduces on
# ordinary bulk domain traffic with repair disabled.
# 
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Composite fan-out: model the unbounded expansion of a composite inbox message.

The original machinery in whizbang expanded a single composite row into
child inbox rows one shot at receipt time. A pathological composite can
yield six-figure child rows from one parent, blowing past the per-composite
``MaxInnerEventsAllowed`` cap in practice and silently amplifying inbox depth
inside the consumer. This demo recreates that shape in idiomatic Jac:

- ``Composite`` and ``InboxRow`` are the two archetypes.
- A ``Carries`` typed edge links a composite to its child rows so the
  fan-out is a real graph, not an id-keyed adjacency list.
- ``FanoutWalker`` walks children off a composite, enforcing the cap
  (depth and total child budget) so the unbounded path is observable.
- ``Reachability`` walker reports the size of the subtree a composite
  expands into, exposing the very number that drove the bug report.
"""

node Composite {
    has title: str = "";
    has inner_event_count: int = 0;
    has max_inner_allowed: int = 1000;
}

node InboxRow {
    has title: str = "";
    has ordinal: int = 0;
    has processed: bool = False;
}

edge Carries {
    has ordinal: int = 0;
}

walker FanoutWalker {
    has processed_count: int = 0;
    has budget: int = 0;
    has cap: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can expand with Composite entry {
        print(f"composite '{here.title}' declares "
              f"{here.inner_event_count} inner events, "
              f"cap={here.max_inner_allowed}");
        if here.inner_event_count > here.max_inner_allowed {
            print(f"  REJECT: exceeds cap, would fan out "
                  f"{here.inner_event_count - here.max_inner_allowed} over budget");
            disengage;
        }
        self.budget = here.max_inner_allowed;
        self.cap = here.max_inner_allowed;
        visit [->:Carries:->];
    }

    can drain with InboxRow entry {
        if self.processed_count >= self.cap {
            print(f"  STOP: budget exhausted at {self.processed_count} rows");
            disengage;
        }
        self.processed_count += 1;
        here.processed = True;
        if self.processed_count % 10000 == 0 {
            print(f"  ...drained {self.processed_count} child rows");
        }
    }
}

walker Reachability {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can measure with Composite entry {
        # Walk every Carries edge off this composite and count the
        # subtree size — the very number that surprised operators.
        count = 0;
        for child in [->:Carries:->] {
            count += 1;
            _ = child;
        }
        verdict = "BOUNDED" if count <= here.max_inner_allowed else "UNBOUNDED";
        print(f"composite '{here.title}': subtree={count}, "
              f"cap={here.max_inner_allowed} -> {verdict}");
    }
}

with entry {
    # A small, well-behaved composite.
    small = Composite(title="small-batch", inner_event_count=5, max_inner_allowed=1000);
    small_child = InboxRow(title="small-0", ordinal=0);
    small +>:Carries(ordinal=0):+> small_child;

    # A pathological composite — mirrors the bug report's six-figure fan-out.
    # The composite is admitted with inner_event_count past its declared cap,
    # which is the exact failure mode described in #584.
    giant = Composite(title="redelivery-storm", inner_event_count=210000, max_inner_allowed=50000);
    # We attach a handful of representative child rows; the walker still
    # reports the declared inner_event_count, which is the bug's surface.
    giant_child_a = InboxRow(title="giant-0", ordinal=0);
    giant_child_b = InboxRow(title="giant-1", ordinal=1);
    giant +>:Carries(ordinal=0):+> giant_child_a;
    giant +>:Carries(ordinal=1):+> giant_child_b;

    root ++> small ++> giant;

    print("=== reachability audit ===");
    root spawn Reachability();

    print("\n=== bounded fan-out attempt ===");
    root spawn FanoutWalker();

    print("\n=== fan-out against the pathological composite ===");
    giant spawn FanoutWalker();
}

