# A maintainer on the project PersonalClaw/PersonalClaw (Python) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:
# 
# Issue #761: Tag merge can create a self-cycle (parent_id == id), the invariant set_tag_parent guards against
# 
# ## Summary
# 
# `merge_tags` can leave the surviving tag pointing at itself (`parent_id == id`) — a self-cycle in the tag taxonomy. Its sibling `set_tag_parent` explicitly rejects exactly this (`raise ValueError("tag_cycle")`), but the merge path has no equivalent guard.
# 
# ## Root cause
# 
# `merge_tags` re-parents the source's children to the target (`knowledge/store.py:1856-1858`):
# ```
# UPDATE tags SET parent_id = ? WHERE parent_id = ?   -- (target_id, source_id)
# ```
# When the merge SOURCE is the PARENT of the TARGET, the target's `parent_id` was `source_id`; this UPDATE rewrites it to `target_id` — i.e. the target now points at itself. There is no self-reference check here, unlike `set_tag_parent` (`store.py:1786-1795`) which raises `tag_cycle` for `parent_id == tag_id` and walks the ancestor chain to block multi-hop cycles.
# 
# ## Repro (on a throwaway copy of the live knowledge.db — never the live DB)
# 
# Live taxonomy has `zfs` (id 2, root) and `homelab` (id 1, parent=2). On a `cp` of the DB, through the real `KnowledgeStore`:
# ```
# merge_tags(source=2 /*zfs, the parent*/, target=1 /*homelab, the child*/)  -> {'moved':20,'already':3}
# tag 1 (homelab): parent_id == 1   # SELF-CYCLE
# ```
# Contrast (asymmetry): live `PATCH /api/knowledge/tags/2 {"parent_id":1}` is correctly rejected with `tag_cycle` (HTTP 400). Only the merge path is unguarded.
# 
# ## Expected vs Actual
# 
# - **Expected:** merge should null the survivor's parent (or refuse) when the merge would point it at itself, mirroring `set_tag_parent`'s cycle guard.
# - **Actual:** a `parent_id == id` row persists.
# 
# ## Scope
# 
# Medium. No hang reproduced today (the store's own `set_tag_parent` ancestor-walk carries a `seen` set, and `TagManager.tsx` renders one level, no recursion), but: the row violates the taxonomy's core invariant; a self-cycled tag is not a `parent_id === null` root so it renders as an orphan "straggler" in the FE (`TagManager.tsx:78`); and any future recursive / `WITH RECURSIVE` consumer of `parent_id` inherits a latent infinite loop — the exact hazard the `set_tag_parent` guard exists to prevent. Distinct from #606 (that issue is merge's missing confirm + unknown-id-fails-open; this is the self-cycle).
# 
# ## Environment
# 
# `origin/main` @ `9285070436b508431519647c898fb09775d5c486`. Reproduced on a discarded DB copy; the live instance was never mutated (baseline 26/154/175, 64 tags verified before and after).
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Tag taxonomy: model the parent-child tag merge that can create a self-cycle.

The issue: ``merge_tags(source=parent_of_target, target=child)`` rewrites the
child's ``parent_id`` from ``source`` to ``target`` — so the child ends up
pointing at itself. The sibling ``set_tag_parent`` would raise ``tag_cycle``
on the same condition; the merge path does not.

This demo models the taxonomy as a graph: Tag nodes connected by a typed
``ParentOf`` edge. A walker that mimics ``set_tag_parent`` rejects any move
that would create a cycle (including a self-loop). A second walker mimics the
buggy ``merge_tags`` flow — it re-parents the source's children onto the
target, which is the exact mutation the issue complains about. A third walker
audits the graph for the invariant that the merge path broke: ``parent_id !=
id`` for every tag.
"""

node Tag {
    has name: str = "";
    has tag_id: int = 0;
    has parent_id: int = -1;
}

edge ParentOf { has since: str = ""; }

walker SetParent {
    """Mirror of ``set_tag_parent``: rejects self- and ancestor-cycles."""

    has target_id: int = 0;
    has new_parent_id: int = 0;
    has rejected: bool = False;
    has reject_reason: str = "";

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can attempt with Tag entry {
        if here.tag_id != self.target_id {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        if self.new_parent_id == here.tag_id {
            self.rejected = True;
            self.reject_reason = "tag_cycle(self)";
            disengage;
        }
        here.parent_id = self.new_parent_id;
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker MergeTags {
    """Mirror of the buggy ``merge_tags``: re-parents source's children to target.

    When the merge source is the parent of the target, the UPDATE
    ``UPDATE tags SET parent_id = ? WHERE parent_id = ?`` rewrites the
    target's own ``parent_id`` from ``source_id`` to ``target_id`` — a
    self-cycle that this walker reproduces faithfully. The fix is *not*
    applied here on purpose: the demo shows the bug.
    """

    has source_id: int = 0;
    has target_id: int = 0;
    has moved: int = 0;

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can merge with Tag entry {
        if here.tag_id == self.source_id {
            visit [-->];
            return;
        }
        if here.parent_id == self.source_id {
            here.parent_id = self.target_id;
            self.moved += 1;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker CycleAudit {
    """Walk the tag graph and assert the invariant: ``parent_id != tag_id``.

    This is the check the issue says is missing on the merge path.
    """

