# A maintainer on the project OrpingtonClose/MiroThinker (Python) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:
# 
# Issue #226: feat(flock): recursive CTE lineage traversal for SYNTHESIZE depth
# 
# ## Current limitation
# SYNTHESIZE queries look at flat cluster membership — `WHERE cluster_id = ?`. A synthesis produced in round 1 has `parent_ids` linking to its source findings, but this lineage is never traversed. Round 2's SYNTHESIZE cannot discover that synthesis S1 shares ancestry with findings in cluster 12.
# 
# ## What DuckDB enables
# Recursive CTEs (`WITH RECURSIVE`) can walk the full `parent_id` DAG:
# 
# ```sql
# WITH RECURSIVE lineage AS (
#     SELECT id, fact, parent_id, 0 as depth
#     FROM conditions WHERE id = ?
#     UNION ALL
#     SELECT c.id, c.fact, c.parent_id, l.depth + 1
#     FROM conditions c JOIN lineage l ON c.parent_id = l.id
#     WHERE l.depth < 10
# )
# SELECT * FROM lineage;
# ```
# 
# ## What this enables
# **Recursive synthesis** — the Flock generates meta-syntheses from its own prior syntheses:
# - Round 1: Synthesize findings in cluster 7 → produces synthesis S1
# - Round 2: Recursive CTE discovers S1 shares parent ancestry with cluster 12 findings
# - SYNTHESIZE combines S1 + cluster 12 → second-order synthesis S2
# - This is the architecture's core promise: the ConditionStore IS the intelligence, and recursive SQL IS the reasoning
# 
# ## Implementation
# - Replace flat cluster query in SYNTHESIZE section with recursive CTE that walks `parent_id` chains
# - Limit recursion depth to prevent runaway (10 hops)
# - Add `synthesis_depth` metadata to track how many synthesis layers deep a finding is
# - Higher-depth syntheses get priority boost (they represent more integrated knowledge)
# 
# Part of: flock-query-depth
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Recursive lineage walker over parent DAG, lifted to typed edges.

Models the Flock SYNTHESIZE scenario: findings form a parent DAG, and a
synthesis must recursively walk its ancestors up to a depth cap, then
produce a new synthesis node annotated with depth metadata. The walker
replaces hand-rolled CTE-style recursion with an explicit depth-bounded
traversal over a typed ``ParentOf`` edge, carrying its own visited set so
cyclic graphs cannot loop forever.
"""

node Finding {
    has title: str = "";
    has cluster_id: int = 0;
    has synthesis_depth: int = 0;
}

node Synthesis {
    has title: str = "";
    has cluster_id: int = 0;
    has synthesis_depth: int = 0;
}

edge ParentOf {
    has role: str = "source";
}

walker LineageWalker {
    has max_depth: int = 10;
    has lineage_titles: list[str] = [];
    has visited_ids: list[int] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can descend with Synthesis | Finding entry {
        if id(here) not in self.visited_ids and len(self.visited_ids) <= self.max_depth {
            self.visited_ids = self.visited_ids + [id(here)];
            tag = "synthesis" if isinstance(here, Synthesis) else "finding";
            label = f"{here.title} ({tag}, d={here.synthesis_depth}, cluster {here.cluster_id})";
            self.lineage_titles = self.lineage_titles + [label];
            visit [->:ParentOf:->];
        }
    }
}

walker RecursiveSynthesizer {
    has max_depth: int = 10;
    has new_title: str = "";
    has seen: list[int] = [];
    has combined_clusters: list[int] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can gather with Synthesis | Finding entry {
