# - `graph build --llm`: buffers the token stream (reusing the `--no-stream` path's approach in `ask.go`), parses JSON tolerantly, then **hard-validates every triple against the seed** — unknown predicate → drop; subject/object type violating `domain`/`range` → drop; label not resolvable to an existing row → drop
# - Run bookkeeping: `extraction_runs.dropped` counts validation failures; the summary reports the drop rate
# - A triple whose last evidence row goes with a deleted chunk is removed — a fact never outlives the text that said it
# - `entity show <name> --hops N` gains the traversal
# - `graph stats`: triples per predicate, rows whose predicate has left the seed
# 
# ## Why it is structured this way
# `internal/llm.LLM` streams tokens and nothing else — no JSON mode, no structured output, no tool calling — and the default provider is a local MLX model. Depending on that model emitting valid JSON several thousand times is not a foundation, so the gazetteer stays the default and this path validates hard. See `33-ontology.md` D4.
# 
# ## Acceptance
# - `make check-ci` green (≥85% per package)
# - `graph build --llm` populates triples with evidence; `graph build` without it still works and touches no LLM
# - Unparseable model output produces zero rows and a counted drop, not an error and not junk
# - Retrieval still byte-identical to before #153 — the expander is the next PR
# - Tests, with a fake `chatFn` returning canned token channels (the `ask_run_test.go` pattern): well-formed JSON; JSON wrapped in prose; trailing comma; unparseable → zero rows + counted drop; unknown predicate dropped; `domain`/`range` violation dropped; stream error propagated; context cancellation mid-stream. `Traverse` at 1/2/3 hops, a cycle (`A→B→A`) terminates, cap honoured, disconnected seed returns empty.
# 
# ## Cost, stated plainly
# One LLM call per chunk. A 5,000-chunk corpus at ~2 s/chunk on a local model is roughly 3 hours. `--llm` is opt-in and `--topic`-scopeable; the docs should say this rather than bury it.
# 
# ## Rollout
# PR 5 of 6. Follow-up: feat(retrieval): entity expansion behind a flag.
# 
# ## References
# - Plan: `docs/plans/33-ontology.md` (D4, D9)
# - Roadmap: `docs/plans/next-steps.md` (#33)
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Validated relation graph with evidence and cycle-safe traversal."""

node Entity {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has name: str = "";
    has seen: bool = False;
}

node Chunk {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    has text: str = "";
}

edge RelatedTo {
    has predicate: str = "";
    has confidence: float = 0.0;
}

edge Evidence {
    has confidence: float = 0.0;
    has run_id: str = "";
}

walker RelationWalker {
    has max_hops: int = 4;
    has hops: int = 0;
    has found: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can follow with Entity entry {
        if here.seen {
            print(f"cycle guard stopped at {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }
        here.seen = True;
        self.found.append(here.name);
        print(f"entity: {here.name}");

        if self.hops >= self.max_hops {
            print(f"hop cap reached at {here.name}");
            disengage;
        }

        self.hops += 1;
        visit [->:RelatedTo:->] else { disengage; }
    }

    can inspect with Chunk entry {
        print(f"evidence chunk: {here.title}");
        visit [->:Evidence:->] else { disengage; }
    }
}

with entry {
    alice = Entity(name="Alice", title="Alice", year=2024);
    bob = Entity(name="Bob", title="Bob", year=2024);
    carol = Entity(name="Carol", title="Carol", year=2024);
    source = Chunk(
        title="Seed chunk",
        text="Alice knows Bob and Bob knows Carol."
    );

    root ++> alice;
    root ++> source;

    alice +>:RelatedTo(predicate="knows", confidence=0.98):+> bob;
    bob +>:RelatedTo(predicate="knows", confidence=0.97):+> carol;
    carol +>:RelatedTo(predicate="knows", confidence=0.96):+> alice;

    source +>:Evidence(confidence=0.99, run_id="run-1"):+> alice;

    root spawn RelationWalker(max_hops=4);
}

