# from: iss_anatolykoptev__go-hooppy__123
# A maintainer on the project anatolykoptev/go-hooppy (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:
# 
# Issue #123: jsonFields: a self-referential embedded type overflows the stack instead of failing a test
# 
# `collectJSONFields` recurses into untagged embedded structs with no `visited` set, so a self-referential embedded type kills the process instead of failing a test.
# 
# Measured 2026-07-30 on `cd2d937`:
# 
# ```go
# type selfRef struct { *selfRef; A int }
# jsonFields(reflect.TypeOf(selfRef{}))   // fatal error: stack overflow (depth ~0x12f67f)
# ```
# 
# Mutual recursion does the same — `mutA` embedding `*mutB` embedding `*mutA`.
# 
# `encoding/json`'s own `typeFields` guards this with `visited[f.typ]`, so this is the last place the helper does not model the decoder it exists to model. Everything else was brought into line in #122.
# 
# ## Why this is filed rather than fixed
# 
# Pre-existing — the pre-#122 `jsonFields` recursed identically — and latent: an AST census over the repo finds exactly **one** embedded field in production code, `doctor.go:184`, a local marshal alias that is acyclic. Nothing reachable from a registered response type embeds anything at all.
# 
# It is filed rather than fixed because #119 and #122 already spent four review rounds on this helper, and the last two rounds changed no diagnostic result on any of the 18 fixtures. The failure mode is worth recording precisely; it is not worth another round now.
# 
# ## What makes it worth doing eventually
# 
# The failure is a **process kill**, not a red test. A stack overflow takes the whole `go test` run with it, so it would read as infrastructure flake rather than as a defect in the type under test — the opposite of what a diagnostic should do when it meets something it cannot handle.
# 
# ## Fix
# 
# Thread a `map[reflect.Type]bool` through `collectJSONFields` and skip a type already on the path, matching `typeFields`.
# 
# ## Acceptance
# 
# - `jsonFields` on `struct{ *selfRef; A int }` returns rather than overflowing, and reports `a`.
# - Same for mutual recursion across two types.
# - Falsify: remove the visited set and watch the test die — and confirm it dies as a **stack overflow**, not as an assertion, so the guard is pinned against the real failure mode rather than a proxy for it.
# - The differential over every type reachable from `liveFixtureSpecs` still reports zero change (45 types today).
# 
# Found during review of #122.
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Cycle-safe JSON field collector.

Models the `collectJSONFields` helper from go-hooppy issue #123, where
recursing into self-referential embedded structs caused a stack overflow
instead of a proper diagnostic. The fix threads a visited set through the
walk so a type already on the path is skipped, mirroring what
`encoding/json`'s `typeFields` does.

The walker below is the idiomatic Jac equivalent: a `FieldCollector`
walker traverses embedded-field edges through the type graph, accumulating
field names on a single typed `has` accumulator. Without the visited set
the walker would loop forever on cyclic graphs (jac 0.36.1 has no
built-in once-per-node visitation), exactly matching the original bug.
"""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entity archetypes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

node GoType {
    has name: str = "";
    has fields: list[str] = [];

    def describe() -> str {
        return f"{self.name}={self.fields}";
    }
}

# A concrete struct type with a list of (field_name, embedded_type_name)
# pairs. embedded targets are resolved via `Embeds` typed edges so the
# graph is the relationship, not a string lookup.
node StructType(GoType) {
    has embeds: list[str] = [];

    override def describe() -> str {
        return f"struct {self.name} embeds={self.embeds} fields={self.fields}";
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relationship: "struct X embeds struct Y by name"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

edge Embeds { has since: str = "v1"; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Walker: cycle-safe JSON field collector
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

walker FieldCollector {
    has visited: list[GoType] = [];
    has collected: list[str] = [];

    def already_seen(t: GoType) -> bool {
        for v in self.visited {
            if v.name == t.name {
                return True;
            }
        }
        return False;
    }

    def mark(t: GoType) -> None {
        self.visited.append(t);
        for fname in t.fields {
            self.collected.append(fname);
        }
    }

    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    # Single union ability handles both kinds of GoType we model.
    # The body's only shared contract is `name` + `fields` (declared on
    # the base) plus the traversal via the typed `Embeds` edge.
    can collect with GoType entry {
        if self.already_seen(here) {
            # Cycle hit: skip this type, do NOT re-enter its edges.
            # This is the fix from issue #123.
            print(f"[skip-cycle] {here.name} already on path");
        } else {
            self.mark(here);
            print(f"[collect]  {here.describe()}");
            visit [->:Embeds:->];
        }
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo graph
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

with entry {
    # ----- Cyclic case: selfRef { *selfRef; A int } ------------------------
    self_ref = StructType(name="selfRef", fields=["a"], embeds=["selfRef"]);

    # ----- Mutual recursion: mutA embeds *mutB embeds *mutA ---------------
    mut_a = StructType(name="mutA", fields=["x"], embeds=["mutB"]);
    mut_b = StructType(name="mutB", fields=["y"], embeds=["mutA"]);

    # ----- Acyclic case: ordinary embedded chain --------------------------
    base = StructType(name="Base", fields=["id"], embeds=[]);
    middle = StructType(name="Middle", fields=["name"], embeds=["Base"]);
    top = StructType(name="Top", fields=["leaf"], embeds=["Middle"]);

    # Wire the edges. Cycles are intentional — the walker must NOT die.
    self_ref +>:Embeds:+> self_ref;
    mut_a +>:Embeds:+> mut_b;
    mut_b +>:Embeds:+> mut_a;
    top +>:Embeds:+> middle;
    middle +>:Embeds:+> base;

    # ----- Run the collector on each entry point ---------------------------
    for entry_node in [self_ref, mut_a, top] {
        print(f"\n--- collecting from {entry_node.name} ---");
        w = FieldCollector();
        root spawn w;
        # Manually seed the walker onto the chosen entry by re-rooting:
        # since walkers spawn at root, we instead just collect by
        # dispatching the same visited/collected logic into a fresh walker
        # whose start fires at our node. To keep the demo single-shot we
        # detach the node from root and re-attach, then spawn.
        root del --> entry_node;
        root ++> entry_node;
        w2 = FieldCollector();
        root spawn w2;
        print(f"result for {entry_node.name}: {w2.collected}");
    }

    # Re-attach the acyclic one for the final showcase run.
    print("\n=== showcase: selfRef must NOT overflow ===");
    root del --> mut_a;
    root del --> top;
    root ++> self_ref;
    walker_show = FieldCollector();
    root spawn walker_show;
    print(f"collected from selfRef: {walker_show.collected}");
    print("returned without stack overflow");
}

