# 
# ```html
# <dl>
#   <dt>Division</dt>
#   <dd>Scientific Instruments</dd>
# </dl>
# ```
# 
# ```html
# <li>
#   <span class="label">photo-technology</span>
#   <span class="value">Albumen print</span>
# </li>
# ```
# 
# The semantic vocabulary may contain thousands of labels, while the number of structural layouts used to express them is usually much smaller.
# 
# We should generate wrappers for these structural relations:
# 
# ```text
# repeated record
#   ├── key node
#   └── value node
# ```
# 
# rather than generate one selector for every observed key.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## Goals
# 
# * Extract arbitrary visible product attribute labels and their values.
# * Support labels that were absent from all schema-generation seed pages.
# * Preserve raw labels and raw values without translating, canonicalizing, or inferring them.
# * Preserve optional section context such as `Technical data`, `Photography`, or `Provenance`.
# * Support the six recurring DOM layouts described below.
# * Keep extraction deterministic after schema generation.
# * Keep the generated schema inspectable and reviewable.
# * Remain backward-compatible with existing stored product schemas.
# * Keep existing configured `raw_attributes` behavior unchanged.
# * Prevent relation extraction from breaking otherwise valid product extraction.
# * Surface relation extraction in schema reviews, demos, fixtures, and diagnostics.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## Non-goals
# 
# * Do not automatically map arbitrary labels to the existing canonical raw-attribute registry.
# * Do not translate keys or values.
# * Do not infer values that are not explicitly visible on the page.
# * Do not derive facts from titles, URLs, image alt text, navigation, or surrounding prose.
# * Do not replace existing fixed fields such as `title`, `price`, `state`, or `images`.
# * Do not create one generated selector per observed attribute label.
# * Do not treat a newly encountered key as schema drift when its structural layout is already covered.
# * Do not silently merge open-world attributes into the existing `raw_attributes` map.
# * Do not make missing optional relation matches fail the complete product scrape.
# * Do not add automatic ontology alignment in this issue.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## Design principles
# 
# ### 1. Structural and semantic schemas are separate
# 
# The wrapper answers:
# 
# > Where is an arbitrary label, and which value belongs to it?
# 
# A later schema-alignment component may answer:
# 
# > What canonical concept does this label represent?
# 
# The crawler must preserve the raw relation even when canonicalization is unavailable.
# 
# ### 2. Relation rules must be layout-oriented
# 
# Good:
# 
# ```yaml
# region_selector: "table.product-specifications"
# type: "table_rows"
# row_selector: "tr"
# key:
#   selector: "th"
#   type: "text"
# value:
#   selector: "td"
#   type: "text"
#   cardinality: "all"
# ```
# 
# Bad:
# 
# ```yaml
# raw_attributes:
#   rawSoundboardMaterial:
#     selector: ".resonanzbodenmaterial"
# ```
# 
# The good rule works for future keys that were never present in the seed pages.
# 
# ### 3. Raw extraction must be lossless enough for later interpretation
# 
# Preserve:
# 
# * raw key;
# * one or more raw values;
# * optional section heading;
# * source relation rule;
# * page order.
# 
# ### 4. Unseen vocabulary is not an extraction error
# 
# This must work without regeneration:
# 
# ```text
# Seed page:
#   Material: Walnut
#   Origin: Vienna
# 
# Future page:
#   Resonanzbodenmaterial: Fichte
# ```
# 
# when both pages use the same relation layout.
# 
# ### 5. Unseen structure is different from unseen vocabulary
# 
# ```text
# unseen key + known layout
#     → extract normally
# 
# known key + unseen layout
#     → relation-layout coverage gap
# 
# unseen key + unseen layout
#     → relation-layout coverage gap
# ```
# 
# Only structural novelty should eventually trigger schema repair.
# 
# ---
# 
# ## Proposed schema model
# 
# Add a new module:
# 
# ```text
# src/crawler/src/scraper/css_selector/attribute_relation.rs
# ```
# 
# Expose it from:
# 
# ```text
# src/crawler/src/scraper/css_selector/mod.rs
# ```
# 
# Extend `ProductCssSelectorSchema`:
# 
# ```rust
# pub struct ProductCssSelectorSchema {
#     // Existing fixed fields remain unchanged.
#     pub shops_product_id: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub title: ExtractionRule,
#     pub description: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub price: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub price_estimate_min: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub price_estimate_max: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub seller_name: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub state: ExtractionRule,
#     pub images: ExtractionRule,
#     pub auction_start: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     pub auction_end: Option<ExtractionRule>,
#     
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Open-world product attribute relation extraction.

Models the core scenario from the issue: a product page exposes an arbitrary
catalog of key/value pairs across several DOM relation layouts (table rows,
definition lists, label/value spans). Fixed fields remain canonical while
relation rules extract long-tail attributes without requiring per-key selectors.

Demonstrates:
  * typed edges for relation layouts (TableRows, DefinitionList, LabelValue),
  * a walker that walks every relation rule and harvests raw key/value pairs,
  * backward compatibility: raw_attributes (closed-world) stay as-is.
"""

# ---------- Entity archetypes ----------

node RelationRule {
    has rule_name: str = "";
    has region_selector: str = "";
    has row_selector: str = "";
    has key_selector: str = "";
    has value_selector: str = "";
    has layout_kind: str = "";
}

node RawAttribute {
    has raw_key: str = "";
    has raw_values: list[str] = [];
    has section: str = "";
    has source_rule: str = "";
}

node FixedField {
    has field_name: str = "";
    has value: str = "";
}

node ProductPage {
    has url: str = "";
    has title: str = "";
}

# ---------- Typed edges ----------

edge AppliesTo {
    # RelationRule -> ProductPage region(s) it covers.
}

edge HarvestedFrom {
    # RawAttribute -> RelationRule it was extracted by.
}

edge HasFixedField {
    # ProductPage -> FixedField (closed-world canonical fields).
}

edge CoversLayout {
    # ProductPage -> RelationRule (the page declares it uses this layout).
}

# ---------- Walker: extract arbitrary attributes via relation rules ----------

walker AttributeHarvester {
    has page_url: str = "";
    has harvested: dict[str, list[str]] = {};
    has visited_rules: list[str] = [];

    can begin with Root entry {
        # Find the product page to start harvesting on.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can enter_page with ProductPage entry {
        self.page_url = here.url;
        print(f"Harvesting attributes from {here.url}");
        # First, walk the fixed fields (closed-world) and report them.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can collect_fixed with FixedField entry {
        print(f"  [fixed] {here.field_name} = {here.value}");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can dispatch_layout with RelationRule entry {
        # Cycle / revisit guard: jac 0.36.1 has no built-in once-per-node
        # visitation, so we keep an explicit list of rules we've already
        # processed on this walker.
        already = False;
        for name in self.visited_rules {
            if name == here.rule_name {
                already = True;
            }
        }
        if already {
            visit [-->];
        }
        self.visited_rules.append(here.rule_name);
        print(f"  [layout:{here.layout_kind}] rule={here.rule_name} "
              f"region={here.region_selector}");
        # Walk to the raw attributes produced by this rule.
        visit [-->];
    }

    can record_attribute with RawAttribute entry {
        # Section context preserved; raw key + raw values kept lossless.
        bucket = self.harvested.get(here.raw_key, []);
        for v in here.raw_values {
            bucket.append(v);
        }
        self.harvested[here.raw_key] = bucket;
        section_label = here.section if here.section else "(no section)";
        print(f"    + raw[{here.raw_key}] = {here.raw_values}  "
              f"src={here.source_rule} section={section_label}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---------- Walker: simulate a future page with an unseen label ----------

walker FuturePageCheck {
    has seen_keys: list[str] = [];

    can begin with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can on_page with ProductPage entry {
        print(f"Future-page probe on {here.url}:");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can on_rule with RelationRule entry {
        # A future page that introduces a never-seen key still works because
        # the rule is layout-oriented, not label-oriented.
        print(f"  layout rule available: {here.rule_name} "
              f"({here.layout_kind}) -> any future label is accepted");
        visit [-->];
    }

    can on_attr with RawAttribute entry {
        self.seen_keys.append(here.raw_key);
        print(f"  harvested unseen key: {here.raw_key} = {here.raw_values}");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

# ---------- Demonstration graph ----------

with entry {
    page = ProductPage(url="https://example.com/guitar/42", title="Vintage Guitar");

    # Closed-world fixed fields (unchanged behavior).
    title_field = FixedField(field_name="title", value="Vintage Guitar");
    price_field = FixedField(field_name="price", value="1299.00");
    state_field = FixedField(field_name="state", value="used");

    # Three relation rules for the six recurring DOM layouts.
    table_rule = RelationRule(
        rule_name="spec_table",
        region_selector="table.product-specifications",
        row_selector="tr",
        key_selector="th",
        value_selector="td",
        layout_kind="table_rows"
    );

    dl_rule = RelationRule(
        rule_name="spec_dl",
        region_selector="dl.spec",
        row_selector="",
        key_selector="dt",
        value_selector="dd",
        layout_kind="definition_list"
    );

    lv_rule = RelationRule(
        rule_name="spec_label_value",
        region_selector="ul.attributes",
        row_selector="li",
        key_selector="span.label",
        value_selector="span.value",
        layout_kind="label_value"
    );

    # Raw attributes harvested (lossless: raw key + raw values + section).
    a1 = RawAttribute(
        raw_key="Resonanzbodenmaterial",
        raw_values=["Fichte"],
        section="Technical data",
        source_rule="spec_table"
    );
    a2 = RawAttribute(
        raw_key="Division",
        raw_values=["Scientific Instruments"],
        section="Provenance",
        source_rule="spec_dl"
    );
    a3 = RawAttribute(
        raw_key="photo-technology",
        raw_values=["Albumen print"],
        section="Photography",
        source_rule="spec_label_value"
    );

    # Wire the graph.
    root ++> page;
    page +>:HasFixedField:+> title_field;
    page +>:HasFixedField:+> price_field;
    page +>:HasFixedField:+> state_field;

    page +>:CoversLayout:+> table_rule;
    page +>:CoversLayout:+> dl_rule;
    page +>:CoversLayout:+> lv_rule;

    table_rule +>:AppliesTo:+> a1;
    dl_rule    +>:AppliesTo:+> a2;
    lv_rule    +>:AppliesTo:+> a3;

    # Spawn the harvester walker.
    harvester = AttributeHarvester();
    page spawn harvester;

    print("");
    print("Harvested open-world keys (raw, untranslated):");
    for (k, vs) in harvester.harvested.items() {
        print(f"  {k} -> {vs}");
    }

    # Future-page check: a page that uses the SAME layout but with unseen keys
    # must not require schema regeneration.
    print("");
    future = ProductPage(url="https://example.com/instrument/77",
                         title="Antique Barometer");
    future_rule_copy = RelationRule(
        rule_name="spec_table",
        region_selector="table.product-specifications",
        row_selector="tr",
        key_selector="th",
        value_selector="td",
        layout_kind="table_rows"
    );
    future_attr = RawAttribute(
        raw_key="Aneroid-Mechanismus",
        raw_values=["Quecksilber"],
        section="Technical data",
        source_rule="spec_table"
    );
    root ++> future;
    future +>:CoversLayout:+> future_rule_copy;
    future_rule_copy +>:AppliesTo:+> future_attr;

    probe = FuturePageCheck();
    future spawn probe;
    print(f"Unseen keys accepted on the future page: {probe.seen_keys}");
}

