# A maintainer on the project aviboddu/NodeScript (unknown) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:
# 
# Issue #29: Deduplicate and optimize the node execution plan
# 
# ## Problem
# `Script.CompileNodes()` builds `NodesToExecute` with a queue and `executionList.Contains(output)`. On converging graphs, a node can be enqueued more than once before it is added to the list, causing it to appear multiple times in the execution plan and potentially run multiple times in one cycle. The repeated linear `Contains` check also makes plan construction quadratic for larger graphs.
# 
# ## Proposed work
# - Build the reachable execution plan using a visited set marked when a node is enqueued.
# - Preserve the current deterministic traversal order for valid acyclic graphs.
# - Ensure each reachable node occurs exactly once in `NodesToExecute`.
# - Add focused tests for fan-out/diamond graphs, including expected output and one-step execution behavior.
# - Extend the benchmark suite with at least one converging graph scenario so scheduling overhead and regressions are measurable.
# 
# ## Acceptance criteria
# - A reachable node is executed at most once per `StepLine()` cycle.
# - Valid disconnected nodes retain their current behavior.
# - Execution-plan construction avoids repeated linear membership scans.
# - Existing successful-script behavior remains unchanged.
# - New tests and the full existing test suite pass.
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""NodeScript execution plan: dedupe reachable nodes via a visited set during traversal.

Models the essential issue — converging (diamond) graphs cause a node to be
enqueued multiple times and execution-plan construction degrades to quadratic.
The walker marks each node when it is first enqueued (not when it is later
recorded into the plan), guaranteeing each reachable node appears exactly once
while preserving the deterministic traversal order over typed ``DependsOn`` edges.
Disconnected nodes that no ``DependsOn`` edge reaches are correctly excluded.
"""

node Step {
    has label: str = "";
    has visited: bool = False;
}

# Typed edge: a Step depends on another Step's output (graph dependency).
edge DependsOn {
    has weight: int = 1;
}

walker ExecutionPlanner {
    has plan: list[str] = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        print("Building execution plan from root via DependsOn edges");
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    # Traversal ability over the typed edge. Marking at enqueue time is the
    # fix for the duplicate-enqueue bug: once a Step is reached via any path,
    # subsequent re-entries (the diamond case) are no-ops.
    can plan_step with Step entry {
        if here.visited {
            print(f"  skip duplicate enqueue for {here.label}");
            return;
        }
        here.visited = True;
        self.plan.append(here.label);
        print(f"  enqueued {here.label}");
        visit [->:DependsOn:->];
    }

    can report_plan with Root entry {
        print(f"Final plan ({len(self.plan)} steps): {self.plan}");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Diamond: root -> A,B -> C, then C -> E. C is the convergence point.
    a = Step(label="A");
    b = Step(label="B");
    c = Step(label="C");
    e = Step(label="E");

    # Disconnected node: not linked by DependsOn from root; must NOT appear
    # in the execution plan (preserves the valid-disconnected-node contract).
    d = Step(label="D");

    root +>:DependsOn:+> a;
    root +>:DependsOn:+> b;
    a +>:DependsOn:+> c;
    b +>:DependsOn:+> c;
    c +>:DependsOn:+> e;

    # d is intentionally orphaned from the typed dependency graph.
    root ++> d;

    planner = ExecutionPlanner();
    root spawn planner;
}

