# The authenticated streaming group export (`GET /api/v1/groups/<slug>/export/`) promises constant, one-chunk peak memory regardless of group size. That promise is false: the `source` section (and the `delivery_artifact` evidence prefetch) hydrate the full `AuditFile.raw_text` — the entire raw JSONL upload, up to `GOGGLES_MAX_DUMP_BYTES` (50 MB) per file — into memory for every streamed chunk, even though the serializer never reads it.
# 
# ## Severity
# 
# HIGH (performance / availability — can OOM the worker mid-stream on a real group).
# 
# ## Location
# 
# - `forensics/views.py:3099` — `ExportSection("source", audit_files_for_group(group), agent_source_row)`
# - `forensics/analysis.py:116` — `audit_files_for_group()` applies no `.only()` / `.defer()`
# - `forensics/views.py:1727-1733` — `delivery_artifact_queryset()` prefetches `evidence_events` / `engine_observations__evidence_events` with `select_related("audit_file")` and no field deferral
# - `forensics/streaming.py:4-6`, `forensics/views.py:3078-3081` — docstrings asserting "peak memory is one chunk regardless of dataset size"
# 
# ## Mechanism
# 
# `stream_ndjson` pulls rows via `.iterator(chunk_size=2000)`. The `source` queryset `audit_files_for_group(group)` has no field deferral, so Django hydrates up to 2000 fully-materialized `AuditFile` instances per chunk, each carrying its complete `raw_text` (`models.py:177`, a plain `TextField`). `agent_source_row()` (`analysis.py:1068-1095`) reads only metadata columns — never `raw_text`. The same `raw_text` re-hydration recurs in the `delivery_artifact` section, whose evidence-event prefetches `select_related("audit_file")` without deferring the big column.
# 
# ## Trigger / impact
# 
# Any authenticated `GET /api/v1/groups/<slug>/export/` on a real group. A group with, say, 50 large files puts ~2.5 GB resident into a single chunk; a larger group OOM-kills the worker. Because the response status is already committed, `stream_ndjson` can then only emit a terminal `{"t":"error"}` line — a silent, partial NDJSON download for the CGKA consumer.
# 
# ## The codebase already knows the pattern
# 
# `forensics/views.py:2819` uses exactly `AuditFile.objects.defer("raw_text")` for the upload-log list, and the admin change form deliberately excludes `raw_text` (goggles#34, goggles#208). The streaming export simply missed applying the same deferral.
# 
# ## Suggested fix
# 
# Defer/limit the heavy columns on every export queryset that hits `AuditFile` (and the JSON blobs on `AuditEvent`):
# 
# - `source` section: `audit_files_for_group(group).defer("raw_text")` (or `.only(...)` the fields `agent_source_row` actually reads).
# - `delivery_artifact` prefetches: wrap the `AuditEvent.objects.select_related("audit_file")` querysets with `.defer("audit_file__raw_text")`.
# - Audit the other sections' `select_related("audit_event")` for the analogous `raw_event` / `raw_kind` / `raw_context` JSON hydration.
# 
# ## Not a duplicate
# 
# Distinct from goggles#201 / goggles#208 (admin changelist/change-form `raw_text`), goggles#39 (upload-log list), and closed goggles#109 (the *old* non-streaming agent export materializing every *event* — a different path that this streaming feature replaced). None cover the new streaming export's `AuditFile.raw_text` hydration.
# 
# Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.
"""Streaming group export: model the constant-memory promise the issue describes.

The naive implementation hydrates every AuditFile's full ``raw_text`` payload
(treated here as a heavy blob) for every streamed chunk, even though the
serializer only ever reads the metadata columns. This demo walks the same
graph two ways:

  * ``NaiveExporter`` pulls every connected ``AuditFile`` node in one shot
    and reads both metadata and the heavy blob together — the OOM shape.
  * ``LeanExporter`` walks the same graph but only ever touches the
    metadata fields, mirroring the fix where ``raw_text`` is deferred and
    the heavy column is never materialized.

Both walkers still traverse the same typed edges (``BelongsToGroup`` and
``MentionedIn``) so the graph structure is preserved; what changes is
whether the heavy blob is hydrated during the walk.
"""

node Group {
    has name: str = "";
    has slug: str = "";
}

node AuditFile {
    has title: str = "";
    has year: int = 0;
    has available: bool = True;
    # The heavy column that the streaming export hydrates by mistake.
    # In the real codebase this is a TextField up to 50 MB; we model it
    # as a string the walker must explicitly choose to read.
    has raw_text: str = "";
}

node AuditEvent {
    has label: str = "";
    # Additional heavy JSON blobs on events the issue also flags.
    has raw_event: str = "";
    has raw_kind: str = "";
    has raw_context: str = "";
}

edge BelongsToGroup {
    has role: str = "source";
}

edge MentionedIn {
    has role: str = "evidence";
}

# Naive walker: visits every AuditFile reachable from the group and
# reads BOTH metadata and the heavy blob on every node. This is the
# streaming-export shape: chunk_size=2000 fully materialized rows, each
# carrying raw_text, blowing the constant-memory promise.
walker NaiveExporter {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can ship with Group entry {
        print(f"[naive] group {here.name} ({here.slug})");
        visit [->:BelongsToGroup:->];
    }

    can serialize with AuditFile entry {
        # The serializer's documented contract only reads metadata, but
        # the queryset never deferred raw_text, so it is resident anyway.
        meta = f"{here.title} ({here.year}) available={here.available}";
        heavy = here.raw_text;
        print(f"[naive]   AuditFile meta={meta} heavy_len={len(heavy)}");
        visit [->:MentionedIn:->];
    }

    can note with AuditEvent entry {
        heavy = here.raw_event + here.raw_kind + here.raw_context;
        print(f"[naive]   AuditEvent {here.label} heavy_len={len(heavy)}");
    }
}

# Lean walker: traverses the exact same graph and typed edges, but never
# reads the heavy blob — the fix the issue recommends. Same walk, no
# OOM-shaped hydration.
walker LeanExporter {
    can start with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }

    can ship with Group entry {
        print(f"[lean] group {here.name} ({here.slug})");
        visit [->:BelongsToGroup:->];
    }

    can serialize with AuditFile entry {
        # Only metadata columns, the way agent_source_row() actually reads.
        meta = f"{here.title} ({here.year}) available={here.available}";
        print(f"[lean]   AuditFile meta={meta} heavy=DEFERRED");
        visit [->:MentionedIn:->];
    }

    can note with AuditEvent entry {
        print(f"[lean]   AuditEvent {here.label} heavy=DEFERRED");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build a small group with a couple of AuditFiles and the evidence
    # events that the delivery_artifact section prefetches.
    g = Group(name="Investigations", slug="investigations");

    f1 = AuditFile(
        title="c2-pcap-2024-01.jsonl",
        year=2024,
        available=True,
        raw_text="x" * 5_000_000
    );
    f2 = AuditFile(
        title="edr-dump-2024-02.jsonl",
        year=2024,
        available=True,
        raw_text="y" * 5_000_000
    );

    e1 = AuditEvent(
        label="process.create",
        raw_event="a" * 200_000,
        raw_kind="b" * 200_000,
        raw_context="c" * 200_000
    );
    e2 = AuditEvent(
        label="network.dns",
        raw_event="d" * 200_000,
        raw_kind="e" * 200_000,
        raw_context="f" * 200_000
    );

    # Wire the graph the way the streaming export would: a group owns
    # audit files (the source section), and files are linked to the
    # evidence events the delivery_artifact section prefetches.
    root +>:BelongsToGroup:+> g;
    g +>:BelongsToGroup(role="source"):+> f1;
    g +>:BelongsToGroup(role="source"):+> f2;
    f1 +>:MentionedIn(role="evidence"):+> e1;
    f2 +>:MentionedIn(role="evidence"):+> e2;

    print("--- naive exporter (hydrates raw_text / raw_event / raw_kind / raw_context) ---");
    root spawn NaiveExporter();

    print("--- lean exporter (defers the heavy blobs) ---");
    root spawn LeanExporter();
}

